[PERSON] (born [DATE_TIME], [LOCATION], [LOCATION], [NRP] SSR, died [DATE_TIME]) was a [NRP] cosmonaut. He was selected as a cosmonaut on [DATE_TIME] (TsPK-6). He retired on [DATE_TIME]. [PERSON] was assigned to the TKS program for a new generation of manned military spacecraft that would be docked to the existing [LOCATION] space stations. He flew as the Commander on Soyuz T-14 to the [LOCATION] space station, for part of the long-duration mission Salyut 7 EO-4. He spent [DATE_TIME] [DATE_TIME] in space. The TKS module was already docked to the [LOCATION] and [PERSON] was due to lead an extended programme of military space experiments. However [PERSON] fell ill soon after arriving at the station and was unable to perform his duties. Although he was originally scheduled to have a [DATE_TIME] stay aboard [LOCATION], his illness forced the crew to make an emergency return to [LOCATION] after [DATE_TIME]. His illness is said to have been caused by a prostate infection, which had manifested itself as inflammation and a fever. He graduated from Higher Air Force School and from Test Pilot School, both in [LOCATION]. He was a Lieutenant General in the Soviet Air Forces, and took cosmonaut basic training in [DATE_TIME]. He retired for medical reasons. He later became Deputy Faculty Chief, VVA - Gagarin Air Force Academy, [PERSON]. He was married and had two children. He died of cancer. He had been awarded: 
*  Hero of [LOCATION]; 
*  Pilot-Cosmonaut of the [LOCATION]; 
*  Order of Honour (Russian Federation); [PERSON]; [PERSON] "For Strengthening Military Cooperation" ([LOCATION]); [PERSON] "For Strengthening Military Cooperation" (Russian Federation Defence Ministry); [PERSON] "Brotherhood in Arms" ([NRP] People's Republic).

[PERSON] (born [DATE_TIME] in [NRP]) is a [NRP] chess Grandmaster ([DATE_TIME]). Plays chess from [DATE_TIME]. First trainer - I. Dambitis. International Master from [DATE_TIME]. He won the Latvian Chess Championship in [DATE_TIME]. From [DATE_TIME] to [DATE_TIME] [PERSON] has participated in more than 80 international tournaments. [PERSON] played for [LOCATION] in Chess Olympiads: 
